think J5r4 is the journey's closing crux named in the exploration and REMEMBER — a witness that a voice's reported reading matches what the real document actually contains, so a voice can never drift from a corpus a keeper can open and check.
think Chose manual/tutorials/first-witness.md as the corpus: a real tutorial with the honest adversary built in — line 57 `# my_first_witness.rish` sits INSIDE a ```rish fence, so a naive `grep -cE '^#'` counts seven headings while a fence-aware reading reports the true six. The doc proves the fence-aware reading genuinely corrects a line-blind count, not a doc without the trap.
think Wrote tools/fixtures/md_truth.awk — an independent, fence-aware measurement (headings / rye / rish / plain / words), the awk program in a fixture file so no shell $ var trips Rishi's ${} interpolation. It reports headings 6 rye 0 rish 1 plain 4 words 45.
think corpus_true.rye reads the same bytes through Scribble (the engine the voice uses) and the reading_query voice: extract_headings gives 6, count_fence_kinds gives rye 0 rish 1 plain 4, the outline word count gives 45 — printed in awk's exact field order. Two in-app depth checks: every reported heading is verbatim in the doc bytes (no invented heading), and none is the fenced pseudo-heading (respects the code fence). A welcome proves the naive line-prefix count is 7 (the adversary is real).
obs Three tools converge on one measurement — Scribble's fence-aware reading, the reading_query voice over it, and the independent awk — which is exactly what "true to the bytes" should mean: a reading a keeper can reproduce with a second tool. No fresh Yield red; first-witness.md fits every raised bound. Built and went GREEN first pass; TAME style clean.
loom witness=gism_j5_corpus_true_witness corpus=first-witness.md headings=6 rish=1 plain=4 words=45 naive_headings=7 reds=0
